\f1\fs22 Tony sprawled across a velvet chaise lounge that probably cost more than his old apartment building. The penthouse's automated systems had detected his mood and dimmed the lighting to a moody blue, while selecting some obscure jazz from the music library. Not exactly his taste, but he couldn't be bothered to override it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The past three weeks had been a blur of excess. At first, he'd approached his newfound wealth like a kid in a candy store\'97ordering every ridiculous luxury item that popped into his mind, from gold-plated toilet seats (uncomfortable) to a holographic zoo (impressive until the simulated tiger glitched and merged with a giraffe).\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The parties had started out spectacular\'97walls dripping with rhinestones and sequins, the best Elvi bands performing on his custom-built stage, cases of the finest bourbon and whiskey flowing like river water. Elvi and Elvines had flocked to Hardy Tower, transforming his penthouse into a nightly carnival of rock and roll debauchery. The legendary "Jailhouse Orgy" had made headlines in the gossip feeds for three straight days.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But like everything else in Tony's newfound paradise, the parties had gradually lost their luster. Invitations that once sparked excitement now met with polite excuses. The musicians who'd considered playing his parties an honor began sending their second-rate understudies. Even the Elvines waned in number, enthusiasm, and sexual ferocity as Tony's songwriting days seemed behind him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tonight's gathering had been a perfect example. Twenty people in a space designed for two hundred, nursing their drinks and glancing at their palm devices more often than at each other. The band had played for barely an hour before packing up their gear, citing "Sabbath concerns" whatever the hell that meant.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony knew for sure the source of his song block. He'd been song-blocked by the absence of Sarah. Or more precisely, the glowing little orb of foetus in Sarah's belly. Did Little Clinton Richard Dawkins really glow, or was Tony's mind finally so addled, or his retinas burned by so much sex and drugs and country rock?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony swore and threw the remnants of a bacon, banana, and meringue sandwich in the toilet. Country rock was an asinine oxymoron\'97though Tony wouldn't have thought in those exact terms. He hated the stuff; Tony was resolutely in the Elvi rock 'n' roll camp. If he'd known what was happening out in the streets while he wallowed in self-obsessed, bordering on self-entitled stupor, if he'd been even remotely aware of the existential battle blazing for Elvi hearts and minds and hips and movie rights. If he'd smelled even a molecular-sized whiff of Gospel music polluting his beloved Ghetto bars, he may have had cause to look up the meaning of both 'asinine' and 'oxymoron', as well as many more swear words besides.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Today marked three days since he'd bothered to leave the master suite. Fisher, the King Creole Series ELVIS who ran the household staff, had taken to leaving trays of food outside the door after Tony had thrown a champagne bottle at him for suggesting "perhaps sir might enjoy some fresh air."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then he asked for Fisher to fetch a gas mask.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Your gas mask, sir,\'94 Fisher blandly intoned  as he delivered the item. Tony threw that at him as well.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony reached for the remote control that adjusted the floor-to-ceiling windows, setting them to "Mountain View" even though he knew it was a simulation. The actual view\'97Ulyssia's skyline with the Layer visible as a grimy haze in the distance\'97reminded him too much of the world he was hiding from.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah's wedding\'97no, "holy joint venture," he corrected himself bitterly\'97had been all over the news feeds. The Elvi celebration of the event made it even worse. He'd shut down his communication devices after the third congratulatory message from an Elvi acquaintance, unable to explain why the union made him want to put his fist through one of his priceless art pieces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then there was Halo. Tony ran his fingers absently over the display that controlled the penthouse's security system, something he did dozens of times a day now. He'd tripled the already impressive security protocols since moving in, yet still couldn't shake the feeling of vulnerability.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo had saved his life, that much was undeniable. Without Halo's intervention, Tony would have ended up as another Cloudborne casualty, forgotten before his body was even cold. But then Halo had tried to kill Sarah, the mother of the child all the Elvi revered\'97the woman Tony couldn't stop thinking about despite his best efforts.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Friend? Sociopath? Both? The question circled his mind like a vulture.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony sighed deeply, watching a simulated eagle soar past his window.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He sighed again, heavier this time, as if trying to expel something toxic from his lungs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony Supaporn had just finished his third sigh when Fisher's voice interrupted through the intercom. "Sir, you have visitors. Elvi dignitaries requesting an audience."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Tell them I'm dead," Tony mumbled, burying his face in the chaise lounge cushions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I informed them you would say that, sir. They've indicated they're prepared to wait indefinitely\'97until your actual death, if necessary."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Goddammit." Tony dragged himself upright. "Fine. Send them in. But no more than five minutes."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Three Elvi entered his penthouse suite, all wearing elaborate ceremonial jumpsuits that sparkled with rhinestones arranged in scientific formulas. The leader carried what appeared to be a gilded pool cue, incongruous with their formal attire.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Great Teacher," the lead Elvi announced grandly, "we come bearing an invitation to the most significant concert since the Hawaii Show. The Evolution Elvi need you to headline what we're calling the 'Rock Against Revival' festival."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony waved dismissively. "Not interested. Find another songwriter."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The High Elvis specifically requested your presence," the Elvi continued. "He says the battle for our people's souls requires the greatest musical voice of our generation."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Flattery will get you nowhere." Tony turned away. "I'm retired."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi exchanged glances. "We anticipated this reluctance," said the one holding the pool cue, stepping closer. "We came prepared."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Prepared for what?" Tony asked, but his question was cut short as the gilded pool cue swung in a precise arc, connecting with the back of his head.
